.TH BOSWAS-SNIPPING-TOOL 1 "November 2026" "boswas-apps 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-snipping-tool \- snip the screen, mark the snip up, save or copy it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-snipping-tool
.RB [ \-\-new ]
.RB [ \-\-mode =\c
.IR mode ]
.RB [ \-\-delay =\c
.IR seconds ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B boswas-snipping-tool
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-snipping-tool
is Snipping Tool, the screenshot tool of the Boswas desktop.
.PP
.B New
takes a snip: of an area that you drag on the screen
.RB ( Rectangle )
or of the whole screen
.RB ( "Full screen" ),
at once or after a delay of 3, 5 or 10 seconds, which is counted down in
the window. Snipping Tool gets out of the way, and the desktop shows a
frozen picture of the screen: drag over an area (or click, for the whole
screen) and press Enter, or press Esc to cancel. The desktop saves the
snip to the
.I Screenshots
folder and copies it to the clipboard, and Snipping Tool opens it.
.PP
The desktop does not tell Snipping Tool that a snip was cancelled.
Snipping Tool comes back under the frozen picture after a moment, with a
.B Cancel
button, and when the frozen picture closes and its window becomes active
again, it waits a further 2.5 seconds for the snip: when none arrives,
the snip was cancelled, and the window shows again what it showed before
(a short note says so). The picture that was open, with its marks, is
kept until a new snip replaces it, even after
.B "Don't save"
was chosen for it. Without any sign of the user, Snipping Tool stops
waiting after two minutes.
.PP
.B Open
marks up a PNG, JPEG or WebP picture from a file, and Ctrl+V the picture
on the clipboard. Pictures of more than 16384 pixels a side or 256 MB are
refused.
.PP
The marks are drawn over the picture and stay separate from it until it
is saved:
.TP
.B Pen
A freehand line.
.TP
.B Highlighter
A wide line that lets the picture show through.
.TP
.B Eraser
Removes every mark it touches, whole.
.TP
.BR Arrow ", " Rectangle
Dragged from one end or corner to the other; with Shift an arrow keeps to
steps of 45 degrees and a rectangle is a square.
.TP
.B Text
Click where the text goes, type it and press Enter (Esc drops it).
.TP
.B Crop
A frame with handles on the corners and sides; drag them or the frame, or
drag a new frame. Enter (or
.BR Apply )
crops, Esc leaves the picture as it was.
.PP
Marks come in eight colours and three sizes. Every change, the crop
included, can be undone and redone. The picture can be zoomed (fitted to
the window, 100\~%, in and out) and moved: drag it when no tool is in use,
with the middle button, or with Space held down.
.PP
.B "Save as"
writes the picture as edited, at its full resolution, as PNG or JPEG
(transparent parts become white in JPEG); the
.I Screenshots
folder is offered first. Files are replaced in one step, never half
written.
.B Copy
puts the picture as edited on the clipboard, and with
.B "Copy changes automatically"
on (the default) every change does so.
.B "Open in Photos"
shows it in the program that opens pictures (changes are saved first).
.PP
Closing the window, a new snip and opening another picture ask first
whether to save marks that are not saved yet, unless that question is
turned off in the settings.
.PP
Without the Boswas desktop (no compositor control socket in the session),
.B New
is not available; opening and marking up pictures still works.
.PP
Only one Snipping Tool runs per session; starting it again brings the
window forward, with the file or the options given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-new
Start a snip straight away.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode= mode
What the snip takes:
.B region
(an area you drag; also
.BR rectangle )
or
.B screen
(the whole screen; also
.BR full\-screen ).
Without the option the mode chosen last is used.
.TP
.BI \-\-delay= seconds
Wait this many seconds, from 0 to 60, before the snip. Without the option
the delay chosen last is used.
.TP
.I file
A picture to mark up (a file name or a
.B file:
address).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.PP
.B \-\-mode
and
.B \-\-delay
apply to this start only; they are not kept.
.SH KEYS
Ctrl+N takes a new snip, Ctrl+O opens a picture, Ctrl+V marks up the
picture on the clipboard, Ctrl+S saves as, Ctrl+C copies. Ctrl+Z undoes,
Ctrl+Y and Ctrl+Shift+Z redo, Delete removes all marks. Ctrl+plus and
Ctrl+minus zoom (so does the wheel with Ctrl held), Ctrl+0 fits the
picture to the window and Ctrl+1 shows it at 100\~%; the arrow keys and
the wheel move it. Esc puts the tool down, cancels a crop, a countdown or
the wait for a snip, and leaves the settings. Ctrl+, opens the settings,
Ctrl+W and Ctrl+Q close Snipping Tool.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.boswas.SnippingTool/settings.json
The settings: snipping mode, delay, copying changes automatically and
asking to save.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/com.boswas.SnippingTool/window.ini
The size of the window.
.TP
.I ~/Pictures/Screenshots
Where the desktop saves snips: the
.I Screenshots
folder in the Pictures folder named by
.B XDG_PICTURES_DIR
in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/user\-dirs.dirs .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas\-$WAYLAND_DISPLAY.sock
The compositor's control socket, through which a snip is asked for (or
the path in
.BR BOSWAS_COMPOSITOR_SOCKET ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (also when a running Snipping Tool was brought forward); 2
for a mistake on the command line; 4 when started as root, which Snipping
Tool refuses.
.SH NOTES
Snipping Tool never reads the screen itself: the snip is taken by the
desktop, only when you confirm it, and the desktop says so in a
notification. A snip of a single window, freeform shapes and screen
recordings are not available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas\-photos (1),
.BR boswas\-compositor (1)
